A self-hostable bookmark-everything app with AI-based automatic tagging and full text search
Alternative to: pocket, raindrop.io
Karakeep (formerly Hoarder) is a self-hostable bookmark-everything app for saving links, notes, images, and PDFs, with a touch of AI for the data hoarders out there. It automatically fetches titles, descriptions and images for links, and uses AI to automatically tag content so it can be found later. It also supports full-text search, browser extensions, mobile apps, and a REST API.
Karakeep Docker Compose example
Self-host Karakeep on your own server, homelab, or VPS starting from this Docker Compose example.
                  It runs Karakeep in Docker containers using the official ghcr.io/karakeep-app/karakeep:release, zenika/alpine-chrome:latest, getmeili/meilisearch:latest images, with persistent volumes and automatic restarts preconfigured.
                  Review the environment variables and adjust them to your setup, save the file as  compose.yml (or  docker-compose.yml), and start the stack with  docker compose up -d.
services:
  web:
    volumes:
    environment:
      # Address of the Meilisearch instance used for full-text search.
      MEILI_ADDR: "http://meilisearch:7700"
      # Master key shared with Meilisearch to authenticate search requests. Must match the value on the meilisearch service.
      MEILI_MASTER_KEY: "changeme"
      # Address of the headless Chrome instance used to fetch and archive pages.
      BROWSER_WEB_URL: "http://chrome:9222"
      # Directory where Karakeep stores its database and assets.
      DATA_DIR: "/data"
      # Public URL where Karakeep is served. Used for authentication callbacks.
      NEXTAUTH_URL: "http://localhost:3000"
      # Secret used to sign authentication tokens. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 36
      NEXTAUTH_SECRET: "changeme"
  chrome:
    command:
  meilisearch:
    volumes:
      # Disables Meilisearch anonymous analytics collection.
      MEILI_NO_ANALYTICS: "true"
      # Master key protecting the Meilisearch API. Must match the value on the web service.
volumes:
  data:
Values set to changeme are required — replace them with your own
                    values before starting Karakeep.
Prefer a managed setup? WinterFlow installs, configures, and updates Karakeep for you using this same Docker Compose configuration.

I have a philosophical beef with “Technology Class” because I feel that technology should be used appropriately in all classes rather than as a stand alone. Truth be told I would like to get rid of all “classes” and “integrate” all classes into each other not just technology.
But I defeat this philosophical problem by trying to make my class project-based integrating all other classes into mine as much as possible. Therefore students do not spend every class in front of a screen, but also build and create things. In the process they write, calculate, research, collaborate, and present. In many ways I think this approach could be a model for how to set up a whole school. Now I do not always do all of these things with every class and I do not always go as deep as I would like either. Which leads me to the title of this post …
I do not like teaching technology class because it is a nine week class at my school. At the end of every quarter we wrap it up, ship ’em out, and bring in a new group the following week. I find it difficult to teach using many tools/methods I would like. For example blogging and Google Docs. It takes a lot of time to set up the accounts and show how to use them properly. By the time students are really getting the hang of them my class is over. Also if I want to have a long-term collaboration with another school it is not enough time to really invest in the relationships.
Last year I got to teach one 8th grade section for a whole semester and it was great. Unfortunately there were a lot of scheduling issues and my class went back to quarters this year. Which leads to the other reason I do not like my nine week class. It is hard to get to know students and build relationships with them. I feel like I am just getting to know my students and they are gone. A few drop by to say “hi” from time to time, but my classroom is isolated in one corner of the building away from almost all of their other classes so I may not even see most of them in passing for weeks.
So when I read other people post about long-term projects and building relationships with their students, I get a little jealous. The best I get is to have the same student for one quarter for three years in a row. How do other teachers with constant “shift changes” build long-term relationships with students?

By Ado Oyeyemi
The Cross River State Police Command has released the photograph of a woman found dead in a hotel in Calabar, appealing to members of the public to help identify her and locate the man who reportedly lodged with her.
The woman was discovered dead in a hotel room along Clifford Lane, Calabar South, earlier in September after she reportedly checked into the facility with an unidentified male guest.
In a statement on Wednesday, the command’s spokesperson, ASP Eitokpah Sunday Akata, said the photograph was released to assist the police in identifying the deceased and locating her relatives or associates.
According to the statement, preliminary investigation established that the woman lodged at the hotel alongside a male guest whose whereabouts remained unknown.
The police said efforts were ongoing to locate the man and determine the circumstances surrounding the woman’s death.
The latest development followed an earlier report by the command on September 8 that an unidentified female guest had been found unresponsive in Room 2 of the hotel.
The male guest who reportedly lodged with her was said to have not been properly documented when they checked into the hotel.
The receptionist on duty was said to have discovered the woman after noticing that the door of the room was open. Police investigators who visited the scene reportedly observed apparent marks of violence on her face and neck.
Her body was subsequently taken to the General Hospital, Calabar, where she was confirmed dead and deposited at the mortuary.
The receptionist remains in police custody and is assisting the investigation, particularly with information concerning the admission of the guests and events before the woman was discovered.
The command, however, stressed that the receptionist’s detention should not be interpreted as an indication of guilt, adding that any further action would be based on available evidence and in accordance with the law.
The Commissioner of Police, Cross River State Command, CP Ajo Geoffrey Ordue, ordered that the investigation be conducted professionally and objectively to establish the identity of the deceased, determine the circumstances surrounding her death and locate the male guest.
The police urged anyone who recognises the woman or has information that could assist in locating the man to contact the command’s control room on 0706 797 7330 or 0905 827 3057, or report to the nearest police station.
The command said further information would be released as the investigation progresses.

