Big thanks to Tools & Twisties! for putting together this deep dive into micro EDC tools. In this video, the host lines up more than 30 tiny, high-quality tools that punch way above their size—titanium utility knives, miniature ratchets, precision pliers, and pocket screwdrivers—and shows how they hold up against a bulky, do-it-all multitool. Rather than one tool that does everything poorly, the idea is a small kit of specialists that actually get the job done, whether you’re a mechanic, a tinkerer, or just someone who likes carrying quality gear. He splits picks into premium and budget-friendly alternatives so there’s something here at every price point.
Items & Gear List
- Pichi G9 Titanium Utility Knife – View at Pichi ($95)
- Pichi X25 Titanium Multitool – View at Pichi ($89)
- Oknife Otacle 2 Mini Titanium Pry Bar – Purchase on Amazon ($30)
- Railer Single-Rail Bit Holder – Purchase on Amazon
- Knipex Cobra XS Mini Water Pump Pliers – Purchase on Amazon
- Workpro Mini Water-Pump Pliers – Purchase on Amazon
- Knipex 125mm Pliers Wrench – Purchase on Amazon
- Workpro Mini Pliers Wrench – Purchase on Amazon
- Würth Mini Bit and Socket Set – View at Würth
- 711L Mini Ratchet – Purchase on Amazon
- 711L T-Handle Extension – Purchase on Amazon
- CROM Mini Ratchet with T-Handle – Purchase on Amazon ($25)
- 711L Pen Driver – Purchase on Amazon
- FWTW Mini Pry Bar – Purchase on Amazon
- Sofirn SE1 Mini Light – Purchase on Amazon
- Wuben E2 Flashlight – Purchase on Amazon
- Caressolove OTF Scalpel Knife – Purchase on Amazon ($17)
- Maxpedition 4×6 Platy Organizer – Purchase on Amazon
- Steinwhale Double-Sided Bits – Purchase on Amazon
- Precision Screwdriver with Bits – Purchase on Amazon
- CountyComm Polycarbonate Pen with Titanium Clip – View at CountyComm
- Felo Stubby Bit Handle – Purchase on Amazon ($15)
- PB Swiss 6464 Multibit Driver – Purchase on Amazon
- Wire Keychain Tool Holders – Purchase on Amazon
- Vessel JIS Bits – Purchase on Amazon
- Oknife U1 Pro Utility Knife – Purchase on Amazon
Another great watch from Tools & Twisties!, and this one lands on a topic every EDC fan eventually runs into: how small can a tool get before it stops being useful? The answer, according to this roundup, is “smaller than you’d think” — as long as you pick the right ones.
The two standouts are the Pichi titanium tools. The G9 utility knife isn’t cheap at $95, but the build quality justifies the “cool tool” label — a lever-actuated blade lock, magnetic blade retention, and a safety switch that prevents accidental opening. The X25 multitool goes even further, packing a ratcheting wrench (rated for M4 to M12 bolts), a pry bar, a saw, a bottle opener, a replaceable scalpel blade, and a quarter-inch hex bit receiver into one titanium body for $89. If you want a taste of that ratcheting-wrench trick without the price tag, the Oknife Otacle 2 pry bar covers the basics — pry, screwdriver, bottle opener — for around $30.
For anyone building a compact mechanic’s kit, the 711L ecosystem deserves attention. The pen driver, mini ratchet, and T-handle extension are designed to work together: the ratchet head detaches to serve as a T-handle, the extension adds reach, and everything accepts standard quarter-inch bits. It’s a genuinely modular little system, and the CROM version offers a slightly cheaper alternative with a nice foam carry case included.
On the pliers side, the comparison between Knipex and Workpro is worth paying attention to if you’re on a budget. Knipex’s Cobra XS and 125mm Pliers Wrench are the premium picks — German engineering, tight tolerances, smooth adjustment. Workpro’s mini water-pump pliers and pliers wrench aim at the same use cases for less money, and while they won’t match Knipex’s fit and finish, they’re a reasonable way to try the format before committing to the pricier originals.
Precision driving gets plenty of coverage too — the PB Swiss 6464 (Swiss-made, roughly $15-20 depending on color), the Felo stubby bit holder, and a stack of double-sided micro bits from Steinwhale and Vessel. The Vessel JIS bits are a nice callout for anyone working on Japanese motorcycles or cars, where standard Phillips bits tend to cam out. Add in a couple of EDC-friendly flashlights from Sofirn and Wuben, an OTF-style scalpel knife, and a Maxpedition pouch to hold it all, and you’ve got the makings of a genuinely useful pocket toolkit that takes up less space than a single bulky multitool.
The overall takeaway lines up with a common theme in EDC: specialized tools that are actually well made tend to beat a jack-of-all-trades multitool for real repair work. Whether you go titanium-premium or budget-alternative, there’s a build here for almost every price point.
That’s a wrap on this micro EDC tool roundup. Watch the full video above for hands-on demos of every tool, including the titanium multitool’s saw and ratcheting wrench in action. Let us know in the comments which pick you’d add to your own kit, and check back soon for more EDC gear breakdowns.

