AFL Draft: West Coast Eagles take latest step in rebuild by selecting Willem Duursma with No.1 pick
West Coast have added another crown jewel to their growing collection of talent, selecting Willem Duursma with the first pick of the 2025 AFL draft.
It came as the Eagles snapped up versatile key-position gun Cooper Duff-Tytler with their second selection - Pick 4 overall - in an expected 1-2 punch at the top of the draft.
Duursma — from Foster, a town of just over 2000 people in Victoria’s Gippsland region — was the first player to have his name called by AFL chief executive Andrew Dillon at Marvel Stadium on Wednesday, having long been linked to the Eagles.
Remarkably, he has become the fourth sibling from his family to break through to the top level, joining brothers Xavier (Essendon) and Zane (North Melbourne) and sister Yasmin (Carlton).
After sharing a pre-prepared handshake with Zane, the 18-year-old was presented his Eagles jumper by a familiar face in rising West Coast superstar Harley Reid, who played with Zane in Victoria Country’s underage sides.
The understated Duursma said he was “pretty happy” to be heading west and joining the Eagles.
“It’s obviously very special, you work so hard over the years and it’s finally a dream come true,” he told Fox Footy.
“Obviously, it’s not easy (to be touted as the No.1 prospect) — throughout the year, you find that out — but just having the support of my family, my mates, that’s really why I’m here tonight. They’ve been a really good help for me.
“That support has really helped me all year and I’m thankful for it.”
When asked if he was ready to form a Batman and Robin-style act with Reid next year, Duursma produced a cheeky response.
“Yeah, if I’m Batman,” he joked.
Duursma has become the fourth No.1 draft pick in the club’s history, alongside Reid (2023), Michael Gardiner (1996) and Drew Banfield (1992).
Forecast as the best player in his class several years out from his final season of underage football, he further enhanced his credentials by being named as the most valuable player for Victoria Country at this year’s under-18 national championships.
A utility who has spent time playing in all three sections of the ground, he was named in the under-18 All Australian side in defence but has also shown the capability to win the ball through the midfield and kick goals when stationed in the forward line.
He got an early look at West Coast’s facilities at Mineral Resources Park when he came up against WA at the venue in June, figuring among his side’s best performers in a 27-disposal display as Reid watched on.
Duursma also proved more than capable of handling the step up to senior football when given the chance to play for Melbourne-aligned Casey in the back half of the VFL season, producing a pair of strong performances which included a 17-disposal, 10-mark debut.
With their second selection, West Coast secured their possible successor to Nic Naitanui by drafting Victorian key position prospect Cooper Duff-Tytler — but not before a pair of unsuccessful attempts to nab academy-tied players.
As had been forecast by The West Australian before the draft, the Eagles launched bids on Gold Coast midfielder Zeke Uwland and Carlton father-son defender Harry Dean after securing Duursma, but were not able to secure either as both the Suns and Blues exercised their right to match.
After seeing their selection drop down the draft board to No.2 to No.4, the Eagles finally opted for Duff-Tytler, a 200cm former basketballer who had represented Australia at under-16 level.
The 18-year-old could finally give the Eagles the long-term answer to their long-running ruck issues, drawing pre-draft comparisons to Fremantle star Luke Jackson with his athleticism and ability to compete around the ground.
After being taken by West Coast, Duff-Tytler said he modelled his game on Jackson and St Kilda recruit Tom De Koning.
“They’re definitely the two players I look at most, similar to my game,” he said.
“They’re two superstars. If I can pull things out of their game, I think that’ll hold me in good stead.”
In a touching tribute, Duff-Tytler’s table featured a framed photo of two of his grandparents.
“They’ve passed now, so couldn’t be here, but they’re always with us and I think they’d be pretty proud of me,” he said.
But he is seen by some recruiters as more likely to develop into a forward, a theory he added further credence to by kicking 3.2 for Essendon’s reserves side in the final round of the VFL season.
Duff-Tytler gave up basketball to focus all of his attention on football in 2024 — which he said was a “tough choice” — and has gone from strength to strength since, being named at centre-half-forward in the under-18 national championships All Australian side.
He showed off his athleticism by finishing ninth in the 2km time trial at the national draft combine in October, out-running a number of smaller players to record a time of 6min21sec.
The Eagles secured the draft’s second pick as compensation for the defection of co-captain Oscar Allen to reigning premiers Brisbane in free agency.

