Full disclosure before you read: DigiERP is our own product, built by Digilite Informatics. This is a buyer’s guide written by a vendor, so read it as one — the questions at the end work just as well against us as against anybody else.
ERP in Mohali gets bought for one of two reasons: the spreadsheets finally disagreed with each other, or an audit asked a question nobody could answer quickly. Either way, the decision that follows is usually made in a hurry, and that is where it goes wrong.
This page is about choosing ERP software in Mohali specifically — what the local education and business landscape actually demands, what to shortlist on, and what implementation really involves.
Education in Mohali: what an ERP actually has to fit
Mohali’s newer institutions compete directly with Chandigarh’s established colleges, so admissions CRM and outcome reporting matter commercially.
Most degree colleges here report into Panjab University, and that matters more than it sounds. A system that produces almost the right examination and internal-assessment format produces a re-typing job every cycle — which is exactly the work you were trying to remove. Ask any vendor to show you their output against your university’s current format, not last year’s.
Where it goes wrong first
Across the institutes we have worked with, the failure points are remarkably consistent — and none of them are on a feature list.
- The fee module meets a real concession. Not a plain fee — a half-year waiver applied mid-term, a scholarship that arrives after the receipt is issued, an instalment plan renegotiated in December, a refund on withdrawal. This is where most education ERPs quietly fall apart.
- Staff quietly stop using it. If the fee clerk and the class teacher cannot enter their own data without a manual, somebody re-types paper into the system a week late, and every report you pull is stale.
- History does not come across. Ten years of admission registers and mark ledgers have to become searchable records. Ask specifically who does that work, how long it takes and what it costs.
- The reporting format is nearly right. Nearly right means manual correction every examination cycle, forever.
- The price moves. Per-student and per-user licensing charges you for growing and for letting your own staff into the system. Ask what the bill looks like in year three, not year one.
Why DigiERP suits institutes in Mohali
- All modules included. Admissions, students, fees, attendance, timetable, examinations, library, hostel, transport, payroll and accounts come together rather than being sold one at a time — so you are not renegotiating every time you need something.
- Unlimited users on one annual AMC. Priced per institution, not per student and not per user. Adding staff costs nothing, so everyone who touches the process can be in the system. That is the difference between live data and week-old data.
- Budget friendly for colleges and universities. Because the price does not move when your intake grows, the number you agree in year one is the number you can plan around.
- Implemented by the people who build it. No reseller in between. The team that writes the code does your process mapping, your data import and your training — including the trip to Mohali.
- Your data stays yours. Exportable at any time, with a full handover if you ever decide to leave.
ERP for businesses in Mohali
IT, pharma and auto — a mix of service billing and regulated manufacturing.
The same rule applies on the business side as on the education side: the module list is identical across vendors, so it tells you nothing. What separates systems is whether they handle your awkward cases — job work, multi-location stock, credit control, batch traceability — and whether the people on your shop floor and in your accounts room will actually use them.
Questions worth asking any ERP vendor in Mohali
Ask these six in the first meeting. The answers shorten a shortlist quickly.
- Who does the implementation — you, or a reseller? Someone has to sit with our staff and import our records. Which company is that?
- How is it priced, and what does year three look like? Per-user and per-student models charge you for succeeding.
- Show me a concession and a refund on screen. Not a slide — the actual screens, with our kind of case.
- Will it produce our reporting format exactly? Bring the current format to the meeting and make them run it.
- What happens during admission week, or at year-end? The system has to hold up on our busiest fortnight, not a quiet demo afternoon.
- How do we get our data out if we leave? A vague answer is the answer.
What implementation in Mohali actually involves
Nobody switches on an ERP. The honest sequence is: map how your institution or business actually works before anyone touches a screen; import your student, staff, fee or item masters from wherever they live today; set up your fee heads, concessions and academic structure — or your item masters, godowns and costing; put your own formats on receipts, challans and mark sheets; then train the people who will use it daily.
A school can be running in a few weeks. A university with multiple programmes, affiliation reporting and years of historical records takes longer, and any vendor who tells you otherwise has not looked at your data. The sensible approach is to go live with admissions and fees first, then add examinations, library, hostel and transport once the first part is running cleanly.
Talk to us
We work with institutions and businesses across Punjab, and we would rather tell you a module is not ready than sell you a demo you will never receive. Call +91 83498 62321 or write to <EMAIL> and we will run your own data through the system, no obligation.
Whatever you shortlist, judge it on your own paperwork rather than the vendor’s slides. Bring a real admission list and a real fee register, concessions included, and watch what the system does with them.
More on choosing ERP in India: the state-by-state guide to education ERP software, or the comparison series — Top 5 · Top 10 · Top 25 · Top 50 · Top 100.

Diablo 3 previews Patch 2.4’s Greyhollow Island
Diablo 3‘s patch 2.4 has been on the PTR for roughly a month now, but today we received an official preview of the new zone Greyhollow Island. Greyhollow Island is described as a zone where story is delivered via exploration rather than exposition — a story is told through journals and landmarks, where each player may have their own interpretation of the events which transpired on the island. That method of storytelling is more viable in Adventure Mode than a cast of characters delivering monologues — the player can decide whether or not they care to delve into the mystery of Greyhollow.
The sting of sea salt burns your eyes, adding insult to injury as an endless storm rages on. The memory of countless ships dashed upon jagged shores is fresh in your mind as a streak of lighting cracks across the sky, briefly illuminating the dense forest that surrounds you. The heavy scent of pine and sodden earth is interrupted by the sickly sweet note of decay, and a thousand flickering eyes in the distance serve as warning—if the madness of Greyhollow Island does not take you first, the beasts surely will.
Greyhollow Island takes its inspiration from the Pacific Northwest. Formerly home to a highly advanced empire, it has collapsed for reasons unknown — leaving behind the evils we’ll explore in patch 2.4. Check out the Greyhollow Island blog post for the full details and the patch notes for more information on patch 2.4.
