cyclonichavonic writes:

The Sheyenne River forms the south border of the Spirit Lake Reservation. What does the name Sheyenne / Cheyenne have to do with the Spirit Lake Dakota? The answer: on the south bank of this river was a Cheyenne earth lodge village near Lisbon, North Dakota. The Cheyenne people today live in the states of Montana and Oklahoma. The Cheyenne name for these indigenous people is not their official name. Their name for themselves is Tsistsistas (Beautiful People).
So where did the name Cheyenne come from? The French fur traders called them Dog people, because when they first met them, they were in what anthropologists call Dog Days. They did not have horses and used big dogs to help them transport their positions. They used two tipi poles crossed on the back of the dog. Then placed tipi covers, robes, and other items across the poles. The French word for this mode of transportation is a Travois.
In the French language a dog is Chien (male) and Chienne (female). Now pronounce the word Chien (dog) with a French accent, and you arrive at Cheyanne, or close to it. The French called the Sheyenne River: Riviere du Chien (River of Dog People). So why the American spelling of Cheyenne and Sheyenne. The term Cheyene designates a river in South Dakota and Sheyenne for our North Dakota river.
The Cheyenne village near Lisbon, ND on the south bank of the Sheyenne, consisted of 70 earth lodges sheltering a population of over 1,000 people. The village was occupied circa 1724 to 1790. The Ojibway and later the Assiniboine attacked them and burnt their earth lodges. They moved west to an area along the Missouri River about were Fort Yates, ND is today. They joined other Cheyenne who set up individual villages along the Cheyenne River in South Dakota, as well as the Missouri River. From this point in time they became plains Indians, abandoned the earth lodge and adopted he tipi as their principal abode.
The Sheyenne River is the longest river within the state of North Dakota. It starts in northwest Highland Township, Sheridan County and flows east some 325 miles to Harwood Township, Cass County, north of Fargo. The Dakota name is Saiya Ozupi Wakpa (River where the Cheyenne Plant their Gardens). The origin of the Dakota word Sahiyena, for the Cheyenne’s name has been lost.
Garcia, Louis North Dakota Place Names in the Dakota Language notebook. North Dakota State Historical Society Archives, Bismarck.
Grinnell, George Bird Early Cheyenne Villages. American Anthropologist, New Series. Volume 20 # 4 1918.
Will, George F. The Cheyenne Indians in North Dakota. Proceedings of the Mississippi Valley Historical Association. 1913-1914. Volume 7 (1914), pages 67 – 78.
Wood, W. Raymond Biesterfield: A Post Contact Coalescent Site on the Northern Plains. Washinton D.C. Press Smithsonian Institution Press 1971.

80 in Indonesia and Other Amazing Things
Fri Aug 22 2025 08:53:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time) 
Greetings from Bali, Indonesia
Last week I was telling you about my trip from Spain to Kuala Lumpur - not without Drama.. if you missed it you can catch up here.
I spent the weekend in KL, one of my favourite
cities in the world, and then on Sunday afternoon took a Batik Air flight from KL International airport to Denpasar, Bali. 
Very smooth flight, about three hours, and very smooth through the airport. Batik arrivals stand is the one next to the arrival hall. Because I have investor status in Bali I get to use the VIP passport channel. Which I enjoy, yes ok I know maybe shallow and childish but so nice to skip the lines. I have to speak Indonesian, because they assume I can (actually not very well at all), but I wing it and like this I'm off the plane and in the luggage area in 3 minutes.. grab my priority luggage and to customs control..   here's a tip you can fill the form and get a QR code before you fly.. customs is just a scanned code and you're outside in a jiffy.  I have Denpasar airport down to a fine art.. and Bondhan is waiting outside to greet me.
We drive from the airport across the dramatic bridge across the bay to Denpasar city.. if you are very lucky you can see Mount Agung which is on the other side of the island but here seems to rise out of the sea in the evening light. I took a picture from the car window.  A nice welcome to Indonesia on their 80th Birthday.. more details below.
Happy Independence Day
So here I am arriving on Independence Day in Indonesia. 80 years so it is a bigger than ever celebration. Every company in Indonesia does some kind of celebration, Red & White flags are everywhere and this massively diverse land is united in celebration. I'm happy to be here at this moment in history.
Indonesia X Factor
AW-Advantage , our digital marketing off-shoot is no exception, and Debbie, our manager, has organised an X Factor-style talent show for the staff. Before work at 9am,  I'm given the honour of being a judge.. Everyone has made a huge effort.. dressed in regional costumes.. they each give a little speech about the culture of the region or island of Indonesia they are dressed up in.  I genuinely learn so much from these guys.  For example I never knew that North Sumatra has a large Christian community with a uniquely developed set of traditions, dances and costumes etc merging Christian and ancient Sumatran elements.    The Batak people have an ancient and developed culture unique in the world like Bali culture is unique.. I really have to visit.
The guy on the left: Edo is wearing the Batak traditional dress. As with all the costumes, every element of the dress holds a particular cultural or spiritual meaning.. these guys explained all this to me.. I learnt so much.
Ok, so young educated Indonesians are super social and super bright.. and our team seems to be more so. I have discussed this before.. Indonesia is an unlikely country.. made up of hugely different ethnicities, religions, traditions, languages - The original multicultural society and yet people in the main get on amazingly together, proud of each other's cultures.
I have only been here a few days and lots happening.. managed to see one or two suppliers.. will report more next week.
Coco is crazy hot.
Meanwhile, the heat bomb in Spain, they tell me, is subsiding, but Coco in China tells me she has never in her life experienced such a prolonged heat wave. The candle factory really cannot operate, the warehouse is too hot to work in during the day. Outside, they have hit 45c.. she tells me you cannot go outside like that. 
But she also told me the notebook and journals that we ordered on the last trip finally arrived in the warehouse. Sandra, our designer in the UK, has worked long hours on the journals, making something truly (I think)awesome - and the dream journals.. fluffy books to write down your dreams in.. gonna be awesome.
Also, from China, we have some Ancient (Chinese) Wisdom.. Excited to see these cultural Feng Shui pieces coming very soon.
They are beautiful and detailed, full of moving parts with masses of meaning.. Take a picture and ask ChatGPT to explain each piece has a story to tell.
When Tomas and I found them, we just had to get them for Ancient Wisdom. 
Meanwhile in the UK.. Holly has been busy this week.
And following on from Interviewing the designer Emily of the award winning designer of the Crystal Elemental Soaps. She went along to see Audrey, one of our amazing artisans at AW-Aromatics, actually making those soaps and other soaps as well.   Behind the Scenes look at artisan soap production.. 
Selenite is Magic...
Holly also gets enthusiastic about Selenite.. and with good reason.. Selenite is a bit weird and wonderful. You probably know we import from a guy with an amazing artisan business in a small town under the Atlas mountains in Morocco.. 
Holly talks Selenite all the details..  Check it out here.
Meanwhile..
In the EU so much new stock is arriving soon.. there is too much to tell you about! 
Enjoy your weekend! 
