Basic research is systematic investigation undertaken primarily to increase knowledge or understanding of fundamental concepts, relationships, mechanisms or observable phenomena. It is not designed around solving one immediate, clearly specified practical problem, although its findings may later support applied research, technological innovation, policy development or professional practice.
Basic research is one of the main purposes for which academic and scientific studies are conducted. It helps researchers explain how natural, social, behavioural and conceptual systems work. Rather than beginning with a request for an immediate solution, it usually begins with an unanswered question, an incomplete explanation, an inconsistency in existing evidence or a weakness in theory.
The term is frequently misunderstood. Basic research does not have to be abstract, laboratory-based or disconnected from society. It can use experiments, surveys, interviews, observations, mathematical models, archival sources, simulations or mixed methods. What makes it basic is principally the purpose of the investigation.
This article explains the definition, characteristics, types, methods, examples, advantages and limitations of basic research. It also shows how to distinguish basic research from applied research and how digital tools and artificial intelligence can be used responsibly.
- Basic research primarily seeks fundamental knowledge or understanding.
- It is classified by its main purpose, not by a specific method or research setting.
- It may be theoretical, empirical, qualitative, quantitative, computational or mixed-methods.
- Basic and applied research are connected, but one does not always lead directly to the other.
- Some studies pursue fundamental understanding while also considering future use.
- Basic research must still be ethical, rigorous, transparent and appropriately documented.
What Is Basic Research?
Basic research is original and systematic investigation conducted primarily to acquire new knowledge about the foundations of phenomena, relationships, processes or observable facts without directing the study toward one particular immediate application.
It is also called:
- Fundamental research
- Pure research
- Basic science
- Discovery research
- Curiosity-driven research
These labels overlap, but they are not always used identically. “Pure basic research” is generally reserved for investigations conducted without a particular use in view. “Fundamental research” is often used as a broader equivalent of basic research.
The OECD Frascati Manual classifies basic research as one of three components of research and experimental development. The other two are applied research and experimental development. The classification is used internationally in research statistics and science policy.
Why Is It Called “Basic” Research?
The word basic refers to the foundational knowledge the research is intended to produce. It does not mean that the study is easy, elementary or suitable only for beginners.
Basic research may investigate highly complex questions, such as:
- How do cells regulate gene expression?
- How does attention influence long-term memory?
- How do languages change across generations?
- What principles govern the behaviour of subatomic particles?
- How do social norms develop and spread?
- Why do some algorithms generalize better than others?
The resulting knowledge may eventually support practical applications, but immediate application is not the central criterion used to design the study.
What Is the Main Purpose of Basic Research?
The main purpose of basic research is to extend knowledge and improve explanation. It may discover previously unknown facts, clarify concepts, identify relationships, test theoretical predictions, develop models or revise existing theories.
Common objectives include:
- Describing a poorly understood phenomenon.
- Explaining why or how a process occurs.
- Testing predictions derived from a theory.
- Identifying relationships among concepts or variables.
- Developing or refining a theoretical framework.
- Establishing principles that can guide later investigation.
- Creating improved methods for studying fundamental questions.
A basic study should therefore make a clear intellectual contribution. “Learning more about a topic” is not a sufficient justification unless the researcher identifies what is unknown, why the gap matters and how the study will improve current understanding.
Characteristics of Basic Research
1. Its primary goal is knowledge development
The central objective is to improve understanding rather than implement an immediate solution in a particular organization, community or product.
2. It begins with a knowledge or explanation gap
A basic-research problem may arise because:
- Existing findings conflict.
- A mechanism has not been adequately explained.
- A theory does not account for a new observation.
- A concept is poorly defined.
- Evidence is missing for a population, context or time period.
- Available methods cannot adequately test an important proposition.
3. It often addresses foundational questions
Typical questions include:
- What is happening?
- How does it happen?
- Why does it happen?
- Under what conditions does it happen?
- Which concepts or mechanisms best explain it?
- Does the evidence support the theory?
4. It may have broad relevance
Basic findings may apply across multiple settings, populations or research problems. However, broad relevance should be demonstrated rather than assumed. Results obtained in one sample or context may not automatically generalize to every other context.
5. Its practical value may be delayed or uncertain
Some discoveries produce applications years later. Others mainly improve theory, methods, scientific understanding or the direction of subsequent research. Not every basic study leads to a product or intervention.
6. It can use many methodologies
Basic research may be:
Basic research is therefore a classification by purpose, whereas qualitative, quantitative and mixed methods describe broader methodological approaches.
7. It is systematic and evidence-based
A basic study still requires:
- A well-defined research problem
- Appropriate evidence
- Transparent procedures
- Valid reasoning
- Suitable analysis
- Ethical approval when required
- Acknowledgment of limitations
- Conclusions that do not exceed the evidence
Types of Basic Research
Pure Basic Research
Pure basic research is conducted primarily to achieve fundamental understanding without a particular practical use directing the investigation.
Examples include:
- Studying the properties of an unfamiliar subatomic particle.
- Investigating how infants distinguish speech sounds.
- Developing a mathematical proof concerning number structures.
- Examining how collective memory develops in communities.
The possibility that findings may later become useful does not change the original purpose of the study.
Oriented Basic Research
Oriented basic research seeks foundational knowledge within a broadly defined area that has potential future significance.
A funding body might support fundamental research in areas such as renewable energy, antimicrobial resistance, artificial intelligence or climate systems without requiring the project to produce a specific market-ready solution.
The research remains basic when the immediate objective is to understand underlying principles rather than deliver a specified process, intervention or product.
Use-Inspired Basic Research
Use-inspired basic research pursues fundamental understanding while also being motivated by a consideration of possible use.
Donald Stokes described this relationship through Pasteur’s Quadrant. Louis Pasteur’s investigations contributed to fundamental microbiological understanding while also responding to practical problems involving fermentation and disease.
Use-inspired basic research shows why basic and applied research should not always be treated as mutually exclusive opposites. A project can simultaneously ask a foundational question and be influenced by a socially important challenge.
Basic Research vs. Applied Research
Basic research develops general knowledge or explanation, whereas applied research is directed toward addressing a recognized and specific practical problem.
| Feature | Basic research | Applied research | 
| Primary purpose | Increase fundamental knowledge or understanding | Address a defined practical need | 
| Starting point | Gap in knowledge, concept, evidence or theory | Problem requiring a solution or decision | 
| Typical question | How or why does this phenomenon occur? | Which intervention or solution works in this situation? | 
| Intended contribution | Explanation, theory, model, principle or foundational evidence | Recommendation, intervention, tool, process or practical evidence | 
| Immediate application | Not the central design requirement | Central to the research objective | 
| Scope | Often seeks broader understanding | Usually focused on a particular problem, setting or population | 
| Common setting | Universities, research institutes, government laboratories and some companies | Universities, government, healthcare, industry, schools and community organizations | 
| Methods | Any suitable qualitative, quantitative, computational or mixed method | Any suitable qualitative, quantitative, computational or mixed method | 
| Example | How does sleep affect memory consolidation? | Which sleep programme improves examination performance among first-year students? | 
The distinction depends on the study’s purpose
The same topic may support either type of research.
Consider academic stress:
- Basic question: How does perceived academic stress influence working-memory performance?
- Applied question: Does a six-week stress-management programme improve working-memory performance among nursing students?
Both studies may use experiments and statistical analysis. The first investigates a relationship or mechanism. The second evaluates a specific intervention.
Basic and applied research can influence each other
The relationship is not always a simple sequence in which basic research comes first and applied research comes later.
Basic findings may inform an intervention. Testing the intervention may then expose an unexplained mechanism, producing a new basic-research question. Scientific progress often involves repeated movement between explanation, application, evaluation and theory revision.
Basic Research vs. Other Research Classifications
Basic Research vs. Theoretical Research
Basic research and theoretical research overlap, but they are not identical.
Theoretical research develops or evaluates concepts, models and logical explanations. Basic research may be theoretical, but it may also be strongly empirical. A laboratory experiment examining a fundamental biological mechanism is empirical basic research.
Basic Research vs. Exploratory Research
Basic research is classified by purpose. Exploratory research is classified by the state of knowledge and the study objective.
A study can be both basic and exploratory when it investigates a poorly understood phenomenon to develop foundational knowledge. Basic research can also be descriptive or explanatory.
Basic Research vs. Experimental Research
Experimental research manipulates one or more variables under controlled conditions to evaluate causal effects. It is a research design, not an alternative purpose category.
An experiment can be basic when it tests a fundamental mechanism or applied when it evaluates a practical intervention.
Basic Research vs. Primary Research
Primary research involves collecting original data directly for the study. Basic research can use primary data, but it may also analyse existing datasets, archives, published evidence or theoretical materials.
Basic Research vs. Experimental Development
Experimental development uses knowledge produced by research and practical experience to create or improve products and processes.
For example:
- Investigating the fundamental properties of a material may be basic research.
- Testing the material for a particular battery application may be applied research.
- Building and refining a commercial battery prototype may be experimental development.
Examples of Basic Research
- Investigating how dark matter may interact with visible matter.
- Studying the molecular mechanisms controlling cell division.
- Examining how ecosystems respond to long-term changes in species diversity.
- Analysing how proteins fold under different physical conditions.
- Exploring the behaviour of particles at extremely low temperatures.
Medicine and Health Sciences
- Investigating how immune cells recognize unfamiliar pathogens.
- Studying the biological mechanisms involved in chronic pain.
- Examining how neural circuits influence appetite.
- Identifying molecular pathways involved in cellular ageing.
- Investigating how genetic variation influences disease susceptibility.
Research involving health or disease is not automatically applied. A study may remain basic when its primary goal is understanding a mechanism rather than testing a specific treatment, diagnostic procedure or public-health intervention.
- How does attention influence the formation of long-term memories?
- How do children form categories for unfamiliar objects?
- What mechanisms connect social exclusion and risk perception?
- How does uncertainty affect decision-making?
- How do emotional states influence the interpretation of ambiguous language?
- How do learners organize examples when developing a new concept?
- What is the relationship between prior knowledge and misconception correction?
- How does feedback timing influence memory consolidation?
- How do students interpret the concept of academic belonging?
- Which cognitive mechanisms influence transfer between related tasks?
- How do social norms emerge within online communities?
- How does occupational identity develop during early adulthood?
- What mechanisms connect residential mobility and community attachment?
- How are trust relationships maintained in decentralized organizations?
- How do people interpret social status in different cultural contexts?
- How do individuals make decisions under ambiguity?
- How do expectations form in unstable markets?
- What mechanisms explain cooperation in repeated exchanges?
- How does information asymmetry influence institutional behaviour?
- Under what conditions do social preferences affect resource allocation?
Business and Management
- How does organizational identity change after leadership transitions?
- Which mechanisms connect psychological safety and knowledge sharing?
- How is consumer trust formed in unfamiliar digital environments?
- How do employees interpret algorithmic management?
- How does organizational learning influence innovation capability?
A study becomes more applied when it is designed to solve a defined problem for a particular company, industry or management decision.
Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence
- Why do some neural-network architectures generalize better than others?
- What mathematical properties influence optimization in high-dimensional models?
- How do language models represent semantic relationships?
- Which conditions make distributed systems resilient?
- How do humans calibrate trust in algorithmic recommendations?
Developing a specific commercial chatbot for a customer-service problem would be applied research or development. Investigating general principles of human–AI trust may be basic or use-inspired basic research.
Basic research also occurs in the humanities. Examples include:
- Examining how a literary genre changed across historical periods.
- Developing a new interpretation of archival documents.
- Investigating the development of a philosophical concept.
- Analysing how translation choices influence meaning.
- Reconstructing patterns of cultural exchange using historical sources.
The evidence and standards differ across disciplines, but the purpose remains the development of knowledge and interpretation.
Paired Examples: Basic or Applied?
| Topic | Basic-research version | Applied-research version | 
| Reading | How do vocabulary and working memory interact during reading comprehension? | Does a specific vocabulary programme improve comprehension in Grade 7? | 
| Agriculture | How do plant genomes change under repeated environmental stress? | Which gene-editing strategy improves drought resistance in a named crop? | 
| Workplace behaviour | How does perceived autonomy affect intrinsic motivation? | Which work-scheduling policy reduces turnover in a particular company? | 
| Public health | How do people cognitively evaluate uncertain health information? | Which message format increases vaccination appointments in a defined region? | 
| Cybersecurity | What behavioural mechanisms influence password reuse? | Does a specific warning system reduce password reuse among university employees? | 
| Artificial intelligence | What factors explain emergent representation in large models? | Which model configuration reduces processing costs for a company’s support platform? | 
How to Write a Basic-Research Question
A strong basic-research question identifies a meaningful gap and asks for explanation, description, interpretation, theory development or theory testing.
Useful question forms
- How does X influence Y?
- What mechanisms explain the relationship between X and Y?
- Under what conditions does X occur?
- How do members of group X interpret phenomenon Y?
- Which theoretical model best explains phenomenon X?
- How has concept X changed across contexts or time?
- Does theory X adequately explain observation Y?
- What dimensions constitute concept X?
Weak question:
What can be learned about student motivation?
Improved basic-research question:
How do first-year university students construct and revise their beliefs about academic competence during the transition to higher education?
The improved version specifies the population, phenomenon and form of understanding sought without committing the project to a particular intervention.
How Basic Research Is Conducted
There is no single basic-research procedure, but most projects follow a structured process.
Step 1: Identify a knowledge gap
Review current scholarship to determine what remains unknown, disputed, conceptually unclear or insufficiently explained.
A genuine gap is not simply the absence of a study in one convenient location. The researcher should explain why addressing the gap would improve knowledge.
Step 2: Define the research problem
Describe:
- What is not adequately known
- What evidence currently exists
- Why existing explanations are insufficient
- Who or what is affected intellectually or scientifically
- What type of contribution is needed
Step 3: Develop the conceptual or theoretical framework
Identify the concepts, assumptions, relationships and theories relevant to the problem. The framework may be used to generate hypotheses, guide interpretation or clarify what evidence is needed.
Step 4: Formulate questions or hypotheses
The questions should align with the knowledge gap. Quantitative studies may test hypotheses, while qualitative, historical or conceptual studies may use open research questions.
Step 5: Select an appropriate methodology and design
Choose methods based on the question—not because one method is assumed to be inherently “basic.”
Possible choices include:
- Laboratory experiments
- Field observations
- Focus groups
- Longitudinal studies
- Secondary-data analysis
- Mathematical modelling
- Archival research
- Textual analysis
- Systematic reviews
- Mixed-methods designs
Step 6: Address ethics and research governance
Depending on the project, researchers may need:
- Institutional ethical approval
- Informed consent
- Secure data storage
- Privacy and confidentiality safeguards
- Animal-welfare authorization
- Conflict-of-interest disclosure
- Community consultation
- Intellectual-property review
“Basic” status does not exempt a project from ethical obligations.
Step 7: Collect and analyse evidence
Use documented procedures suited to the evidence and research question. Analysis may involve statistical testing, model comparison, qualitative coding, interpretive analysis, simulation, logical reasoning or a combination of approaches.
Step 8: Interpret the contribution carefully
Explain:
- What the findings add
- Which theory or concept is supported, revised or questioned
- What alternative explanations remain
- How far the findings can be generalized or transferred
- What limitations affect interpretation
- Which new questions should be investigated
Methods Used in Basic Research
Quantitative basic research may measure variables, test theoretical predictions, estimate relationships or compare models.
- Controlled experiments
- Correlational studies
- Longitudinal datasets
- Psychometric analysis
- Biological measurement
- Computational modelling
- Network analysis
Qualitative basic research may investigate meaning, interpretation, experience, social process or concept formation.
- In-depth interviews
- Participant observation
- Discourse analysis
- Historical analysis
- Grounded theory
Mixed-methods basic research combines quantitative and qualitative evidence to develop a more complete explanation.
For example, a researcher might use a survey to identify patterns in academic belonging and interviews to examine how students interpret the experiences underlying those patterns.
Conceptual and theoretical methods
Some basic research develops arguments, models, typologies, definitions or formal proofs without collecting conventional participant data.
Conceptual work must still be systematic. It should clearly state its assumptions, evidence base, reasoning process and relationship to existing scholarship.
Advantages and Importance of Basic Research
It expands human knowledge
The most direct contribution of basic research is improved understanding of nature, society, behaviour, culture, technology and abstract systems.
It develops and tests theories
Theories allow researchers to organize evidence, explain patterns and make predictions. Basic research can create new theories or determine where existing ones need revision.
It supports future applied research
Applied projects often depend on prior knowledge about mechanisms, relationships, measurement and context. Without an adequate foundation, interventions may target the wrong cause or produce effects that are poorly understood.
It can generate unexpected discoveries
Because basic research is not limited to delivering one predetermined product, it may reveal relationships or phenomena that were not initially anticipated.
It improves research methods
Fundamental methodological research can produce better measurement instruments, statistical procedures, algorithms, experimental techniques and conceptual frameworks.
It contributes to research training
Basic projects help students and early-career researchers learn how to formulate questions, evaluate evidence, analyse uncertainty and contribute to scholarly debates.
It may create broad spillover benefits
Knowledge produced for one discipline may later influence several unrelated applications. Because those future uses are uncertain and may benefit many organizations, public institutions and universities frequently play an important role in supporting basic research.
Limitations and Challenges of Basic Research
Applications may not be immediate
The findings may not produce an intervention, policy or product in the short term. This can make the work difficult to justify to audiences seeking immediate results.
Outcomes are uncertain
A well-designed study may reject its hypothesis, produce an inconclusive result or reveal that the original model was inadequate. These outcomes can still be scientifically valuable when reported transparently.
Funding can be difficult to secure
Projects with easily described economic, clinical or organizational benefits may appear more attractive to some funders. Basic researchers must clearly explain the importance of the knowledge gap and the likely contribution to a field.
Generalization is not automatic
A project may seek general knowledge but use a narrow sample, model organism, dataset or cultural setting. Researchers should not describe findings as universal without sufficient evidence.
Translation can be slow or unsuccessful
A result established under controlled conditions may not produce the same effects in complex real-world environments. Additional applied, translational and replication research may be necessary.
Specialized infrastructure may be required
Some projects require advanced laboratories, research facilities, datasets, archives, computing resources or highly specialized expertise.
Ethical concerns still apply
Fundamental inquiry may involve human participants, animals, sensitive data, dual-use knowledge or environmental risks. The absence of an immediate commercial goal does not remove these concerns.
How to Identify Whether a Study Is Basic Research
Ask the following questions.
1. What is the study’s primary objective?
If the objective is to explain, understand, describe or test a general mechanism or theory, it is likely basic.
If the objective is to fix, improve, design, evaluate or implement something for a specific practical need, it is likely applied.
2. What triggered the project?
Basic research usually begins with a gap in knowledge or explanation. Applied research usually begins with a recognized problem requiring action.
3. How are the expected outcomes described?
Indicators of basic research include:
- New theoretical insight
- Better conceptual understanding
- Explanation of a mechanism
- Evidence supporting or challenging a theory
- New foundational data
- Improved scientific method
Indicators of applied research include:
- A tested intervention
- A policy recommendation
- A product or process improvement
- A programme evaluation
- A decision for a specific organization
- A solution for a defined population
4. Is a specific use built into the research design?
Possible usefulness alone does not make a study applied. The important question is whether delivering that use is an explicit and central design objective.
5. Does the project pursue both understanding and use?
The project may be use-inspired basic research. In that case, the proposal should state both goals rather than forcing the study into an inaccurate binary category.
Basic Research in Modern Research Systems
In health and related fields, translational research connects laboratory findings, clinical investigation and implementation in practice.
The relationship should not be understood as a perfectly straight line. Clinical findings may expose new biological questions, while basic discoveries may require years of testing before they can support patient care.
Modern fundamental questions frequently require expertise from several fields. Climate research may combine physics, biology, economics and data science. AI research may combine computer science, linguistics, psychology, mathematics and philosophy.
Interdisciplinary work should define how the disciplines genuinely contribute rather than merely listing them.
Open-science practices can strengthen basic research through:
- Preregistration when appropriate
- Open research materials
- Transparent analytical code
- Data-sharing plans
- Reproducible computational environments
- Registered reports
- Clear reporting of negative and inconclusive results
Not all data can be made public. Privacy, participant consent, intellectual property, cultural ownership and security may require controlled access.
Reproducibility and replication
Basic findings can influence entire areas of later research, so weak or irreproducible foundations may waste substantial resources.
Researchers should document decisions, test robustness, distinguish exploratory from confirmatory analysis and avoid presenting one uncertain result as an established principle.
Digital Research Tools and Artificial Intelligence
Digital tools can support basic research at almost every stage.
Literature discovery and organization
Researchers may use:
- Bibliographic databases
- Citation-management software
- Systematic-review platforms
- Citation-network tools
- Research-alert services
- Semantic literature search
Searches should be documented using databases, dates, keywords and inclusion criteria when reproducibility matters.
Data collection and management
Digital platforms can support:
- Online surveys
- Experimental tasks
- Laboratory instruments
- Audio or video recording
- Electronic research notebooks
- Secure data repositories
- Version control
Depending on the field, researchers may use:
- Statistical software
- Qualitative coding software
- Programming languages
- Simulation environments
- Geographic information systems
- Network-analysis tools
- Computer-assisted text analysis
Software does not determine the research purpose. The same statistical or qualitative package may be used in both basic and applied projects.
Responsible use of generative AI
Generative AI may assist with brainstorming search terms, explaining code, creating preliminary summaries, translating researcher-written material or identifying possible analytical approaches.
However, researchers should not treat AI output as verified evidence. Generative systems can produce:
- Fabricated references
- Incorrect summaries
- Hidden bias
- Unreliable calculations
- Inconsistent coding
- Confidentiality risks
- Unclear intellectual-property status
- Outputs that are difficult to reproduce
Researchers should:
- Verify every citation against the original source.
- Avoid entering confidential or identifiable data into unapproved systems.
- Preserve records of important prompts, settings and versions.
- Independently validate generated code and analysis.
- Follow institutional, funder and journal policies.
- Disclose material AI use when required.
- Retain human responsibility for all claims, interpretations and decisions.
AI tools should support—not replace—domain expertise, critical reading and methodological judgment.
Ethical and Quality Standards
A strong basic-research project should demonstrate the following.
The proposal should identify the exact knowledge, theory, concept, mechanism or method that the study will improve.
The research question, evidence, sampling strategy, design and analysis must fit one another.
Appropriate validity or trustworthiness
Quantitative studies should consider issues such as measurement validity, bias, precision and model assumptions.
Qualitative studies should consider credibility, reflexivity, transparency, context and the adequacy of interpretation.
Researchers should report inconclusive findings, limitations and alternative explanations rather than presenting stronger conclusions than the evidence supports.
The project must follow applicable requirements for participants, animals, data, communities, environments and dual-use knowledge.
Readers should be able to understand how evidence was produced and how conclusions were reached.
Mistake 1: Calling all university research basic
Universities conduct basic, applied, translational and developmental research. Institutional location does not determine the category.
Mistake 2: Assuming basic research must take place in a laboratory
Basic research can occur in communities, archives, natural environments, online spaces, libraries or computational systems.
Mistake 3: Treating qualitative research as applied
Qualitative methods can investigate foundational questions about meaning, identity, interpretation, culture and social processes.
Mistake 4: Treating experiments as automatically basic
An experiment evaluating a specific intervention is applied, while an experiment investigating a general cognitive mechanism may be basic.
Mistake 5: Claiming the research has no purpose
Basic research has a clear purpose: knowledge development. It lacks a particular immediate application as its central objective, not an intellectual or scientific objective.
Mistake 6: Describing possible future usefulness as a guaranteed impact
Researchers should distinguish a plausible future implication from an application that has already been demonstrated.
Mistake 7: Using “basic,” “pure,” “fundamental” and “theoretical” without clarification
Define the terminology used in the proposal, especially when institutional or funding definitions differ.
Mistake 8: Ignoring research ethics because no product is being developed
Fundamental studies can create significant risks and therefore require the same careful ethical assessment as other research.
Basic research is systematic investigation designed primarily to develop fundamental knowledge, concepts, explanations, theories or methods rather than solve one immediate practical problem. It may use any appropriate methodology and may occur in almost any discipline or setting.
Its defining feature is the purpose of the study. Some projects are purely curiosity-driven, some investigate a broadly important area, and others pursue fundamental understanding while considering possible use. Recognizing these distinctions helps students classify research accurately, write stronger proposals and explain how foundational knowledge contributes to later scientific and social progress.
- Akcigit, U., Hanley, D., & Serrano-Velarde, N. (2021). Back to basics: Basic research spillovers, innovation policy, and growth. The Review of Economic Studies, 88(1), 1–43. https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdaa061
- Bush, V. (1945). Science, the endless frontier: A report to the President. U.S. Government Printing Office. https://www.nsf.gov/about/history/EndlessFrontier_w.pdf
- National Center for Science and Engineering Statistics. (2025). Definitions of research and development: An annotated compilation of official sources. National Science Foundation. https://ncses.nsf.gov/randdef
- National Research Council. (2004). Science, medicine, and animals. The National Academies Press. https://doi.org/10.17226/10733
- Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development. (2015). Frascati manual 2015: Guidelines for collecting and reporting data on research and experimental development. OECD Publishing. https://doi.org/10.1787/9789264239012-en
- Stokes, D. E. (1997). Pasteur’s quadrant: Basic science and technological innovation. Brookings Institution Press.

Hannah Bourne-Taylor - Nature Needs You
Join author and environmental campaigner Hannah Bourne-Taylor for a candid conversation about finding your voice and fighting for the natural world, the subject of her book Nature Needs You. Alongside discussing her grassroots crusade to save the UK’s plummeting swift population – from high-profile campaigning to demanding mandatory swift bricks in new builds – she will dive into her life as an activist and her experiences behind the scenes on Clarkson’s Farm. A frank discussion about rural life, battling bureaucracy and why protecting our wildlife requires radical resistance. She talks to writer and conservationist Keggie Carew.
| When: Saturday 7 November 2026 @ 4.30 pm | 
| Where: Bridport Arts Centre | 
| Sponsor: Rob & Alex Appleby |

Stena Line is disappointed by the announcement today (25 June) that the reopening of Terminal 3 (T3) at Holyhead Port will be delayed for two weeks.
Whilst Stena Line has operated a full sailing schedule since the reopening of Terminal 5 in January of this year, timetable adjustments have been necessary as T3 remained closed for essential repairs.
Johan Edelman, Stena Line Trade Director (Irish Sea South) said: “Whilst we are disappointed that we cannot return to our normal sailing schedule, we recognise and appreciate the scale and complexity of the specialist work that has been undertaken since December 2024.”
“Stena Line has been able to continue to operate its service on the Holyhead – Dublin route with the same frequency via adjustments to the timetable. Stena Line had planned to return to the normal timetable at the start of July, as advised, so I would like to apologise to our customers for any inconvenience and thank them for their understanding.
“Our customer service teams will be in contact with our customers to minimise any disruption and we look forward to the completion of works and a return to our normal schedule on the 15 July.”

