An ensuite bathroom should offer convenience, while a luxury bathroom should provide comfort and relaxation. Combining these two concepts creates a space that feels practical yet refined. A well-planned Ealing Ensuite & Luxury Bathroom design focuses on layout, materials, lighting, and comfort features that improve everyday living. At Living Bathrooms, we help homeowners create bathrooms that deliver both style and functionality.
Ensuite bathrooms are often smaller than main bathrooms, which makes efficient planning essential. Every element must serve a purpose while maintaining a balanced appearance. Wall-mounted fixtures, floating furniture, and integrated storage solutions help maximise available space without making the room feel crowded.
Luxury design begins with material selection. High-quality surfaces such as porcelain, stone-effect finishes, and premium cabinetry create a sophisticated appearance. Consistent colours and textures help establish a calm and welcoming atmosphere throughout the room.
Lighting plays a significant role in creating a luxury feel. Rather than relying on a single fitting, a layered lighting approach can dramatically improve the overall experience. Mirror lighting, recessed ceiling fixtures, and subtle feature lighting all contribute to a comfortable environment.
Storage should be integrated into the design from the beginning. Recessed shelving, vanity drawers, and mirrored cabinets provide practical solutions without disrupting the overall appearance. A clutter-free bathroom feels more spacious and organised.
Comfort features are another hallmark of luxury bathrooms. Underfloor heating, heated towel rails, and high-quality shower systems help elevate the daily experience. These additions improve usability while maintaining a clean and elegant look.
Shower design often becomes the focal point of an ensuite. Frameless glass screens, concealed controls, and rainfall shower heads create a modern and sophisticated appearance. If space allows, a compact freestanding bath can introduce an additional sense of luxury.
Ventilation remains important, even in high-end bathrooms. Effective extraction systems help maintain air quality and protect materials from excess moisture. This supports long-term durability and keeps the bathroom feeling fresh.
A successful Ealing Ensuite & Luxury Bathroom project balances practicality with visual appeal. By carefully selecting materials, planning storage, and integrating comfort-focused features, homeowners can create a space that supports daily routines while providing a relaxing retreat.
At Living Bathrooms, we work closely with clients to design ensuite and luxury bathrooms that reflect their needs and style preferences.
Contact Living Bathrooms today and start planning a bathroom that combines convenience, comfort, and timeless design.

About the product
Seal skins are graded into qualities A, B, C, D, E & discarded skins. “A” skin = top quality (very rare). “E” quality = skin with many flaws. PLEASE NOTE: All our product photos of seal skin here on the webshop are taken from C or B skins. Colour tones vary from skin to skin. Sizes vary from skin to skin. It is NOT the skin you see in the product photo that you are purchasing. Do you have special requests for a skin? – then simply write it in the comments of your order, and we will do our best to find a suitable skin – but we offer no guarantees on requests.
Quality descriptions: A,B,C,D,E and discarded. A-skins have a maximum of two flaws. B-skins can have up to eight flaws. C-skins can have several flaws, as well as one or two larger flaws. D-skins can have areas completely without hair or hard leather. E and discarded skins are typically very damaged skins and with discolourations, which can only be used for the production of very small items, after which the rest of the skin is completely discarded. In Great Greenland’s own production of finished goods, we primarily work with C and D qualities.
Seals live wild, which is why “damage” often occurs from nature’s side, degrading the skin quality. However, this is also what makes each individual seal skin completely unique! The quality specifications therefore tell something about the general condition of the skin. How perfect, or imperfect, the skin is. Read more about seal skin here:
Dyed seal skin: At Great Greenland, we primarily sell natural skins, but we also dye many skins in popular and fashionable colours. Dyeing only comes into consideration if the natural skin is severely discoloured. Dyeing thus saves the skin from being discarded, ensuring sustainable production. A beautiful natural skin will never be dyed if there is no need for it.
Decorative or craft skins? Seal skins are extremely decorative. Can be used, for example, as rugs, as cushions in chairs or sofas. Only imagination sets the limits. Seal skin behaves like other leather goods concerning wear and tear and maintenance. A wonderful material for crafting.
All seal skins processed by Great Greenland are caught by Greenlandic Inuit hunters along the Greenlandic coast. All skins are tanned at Great Greenland’s tannery in South Greenland. All seal skins comply with EU legislation regarding seal skins. Only Inuit-hunted seal skins are approved for sale, production, and import into the EU. See more here on
the EU’s official website:
Harp Seal/Greenland Seal:
Harp seal is also called Greenland seal or silk seal. The name ‘silk seal’ is a very apt description for the appearance of the skin – it is glossy and soft, and it shines like silk. The Harp seal is primarily used for clothing, but it is also extremely decorative for interiors. The Harp seal is characterised by its natural grey colour and the sporadic spots scattered across the skin. Some seal skins have many spots, some have few.
PLEASE NOTE: All our skin product photos here on the webshop are taken from B & C skin qualities. The seal skins are all unique, and can therefore vary in both size and colour nuances within the same quality group.

Chips instead of peppers: Hungary's economy in the European division of labor
European Union & Euro
The article deals with the changing Hungarian trade patterns in the course of transition and integration into the European Union since the early nineties until EU full membership. Although Hungary is far from being a “Tiger economy”, Hungarian export-led growth has been sufficient to catch-up visibly with EU core members. The statistical analysis and a gravity model of Hungarian trade relations reveal that the country has been successfully integrated into the EU Common Market. However, the degree of integration with the other members varies substantially, notably for Hungarian exports. Germany is by far the dominant partner among the former EU-15 countries; furthermore, close trade relations exist with the Visegrad partners. The product mix of Hungarian trade flows also reflects the countries’ successful integration into international value-added chains. Apparently, Hungarian enterprises do no longer play the role of mere workbenches for labour-intensive standardized products. Instead, the analysis shows that the technology content of Hungarian trade has increased significantly, indicating the Hungarian participation in advanced European production networks. Particularly, in branches which attracted substantial amounts of foreign direct investment from Western Europe, Hungarian enterprises improved their competitive position in the technological hierarchy of European markets.

