Sydney Opera House
Mentioned on Performing arts theaterSights & LandmarksThe Sydney Opera House is an iconic arts center located on Bennelong Point in Sydney Harbour, offering breathtaking views of the harbor, bridge, and city skyline. With over 1,600 performances annually, it hosts a diverse range of events including opera, theatre, music, dance, and comedy shows. This architectural marvel is a must-see attraction that draws millions of visitors each year. Its unique design and cultural significance make it a standout feature of the picturesque Sydney Harbour.4.8(92822)•4.7(25540)Bennelong Point, Sydney NSW 2000, Australia
Sydney Harbour Bridge
Mentioned on BridgeSights & LandmarksPoints of Interest & LandmarksSydney Harbour Bridge, an iconic structure opened in 1932, offers the thrilling BridgeClimb experience and the Pylon Lookout with exhibitions. Beyond its fame, Sydney's surroundings offer unique experiences like camping beneath the bridge or glamping in a zoo with a harbor view. The city's wild heart extends to camping areas and caravan parks near the CBD, while train rides from Central Station lead to the Blue Mountains or coastal beaches.4.8(21108)•4.7(14085)Sydney Hbr Brg, Sydney NSW, Australia
Fig Tree Lawn
Mentioned on Event venueNature & ParksGardensLocated in the heart of Sydney, Fig Tree Lawn - Royal Botanic Garden is a sprawling 30-hectare oasis featuring a diverse array of native and exotic plants and flowers. Established in 1816, it is Australia's oldest botanical garden and offers unobstructed views of iconic landmarks such as the Sydney Opera House. Visitors can explore hothouses showcasing palms and ferns, as well as the Calyx, an impressive glasshouse gallery hosting temporary plant-themed exhibitions.
Bondi to Coogee Walk
Mentioned on Nature & ParksTour operatorHiking TrailsThe Bondi to Coogee Walk is a 6 km coastal stroll that offers breathtaking views of beaches, cliffs, and parks. The paved pathway makes it easy for visitors to navigate the route, which typically takes around two to three hours to complete. Along the way, there are numerous opportunities to stop at cafes and viewpoints for photos or simply bask in the stunning scenery.
Mentioned on Sights & LandmarksPiers & BoardwalksCircular Quay, pronounced as "key," is a must-visit destination in Sydney, featuring iconic landmarks like the Opera House and Sydney Harbour Bridge. The area offers stunning views and easy access to various attractions. The nearby Four Seasons Hotel Sydney provides convenient accommodation with top-floor suites offering panoramic views of the city. Visitors can also take a ferry from Circular Quay to explore Manly's beautiful beaches, such as Shelly Beach, known for its marine reserve and diverse marine life.4.5(4394)Sydney NSW 2000, Australia
Circular Quay Wharf 3
Mentioned on Ferry terminalTransportationFerriesCircular Quay Wharf 3 is a bustling hub for ferry services, primarily catering to those heading to the picturesque Manly Beach. With frequent departures and an organized boarding process, travelers can easily swipe through Opal gates and find seating options for those who need it. The journey itself offers breathtaking views of Sydney's iconic landmarks like the Sydney Harbour Bridge and the Opera House, making it not just a means of transport but also a delightful experience.
Art Gallery of New South Wales
Mentioned on Art galleryMuseumsThe Art Gallery of New South Wales, established in 1871, is a top-rated attraction for art enthusiasts. It houses an extensive and diverse collection of contemporary, modern, indigenous, Australian, Aboriginal, European, and Asian art. Located just a short walk from the city center near the Royal Botanic Gardens, this classical-style building welcomes over a million visitors annually. With more than 40,000 captivating works on display and ever-changing exhibitions, it offers something for everyone.Beautiful free art museum in NSW. The Art Gallery of New South Wales is one of the best places to visit in Sydney, whether you love art or just want a peaceful, inspiring afternoon. The building is beautiful, the atmosphere is calm, and the collection is surprisingly rich for a museum with free entry. I loved the mix of classic, modern, Australian, Aboriginal, and international art. The new Sydney Modern section is especially impressive, with open spaces, interesting architecture, and thoughtful exhibitions. It feels welcoming rather than intimidating, which makes it easy to wander, slow down, and enjoy the work at your own pace. The location near the Botanic Garden also makes it perfect for combining with a walk by the harbour. A beautiful cultural space, generous to the public, and absolutely worth visiting. One of Sydney’s quiet treasures.Bulat F — Google reviewFree entry, massive and diverse collection, with a good representation of local and regional cultures. I can't imagine being able to properly see everything there is to offer in a single visit (and we did not try). There are also a ton of outdoor areas with some displays, though many of the smaller outdoors areas are dead ends. Ample parents facilities in both buildings.KJ X — Google reviewI didn’t know much about Australian artists beforehand, so I wasn’t sure what to expect from my visit to the museum. The architecture itself is beautiful and elegant, and I was pleasantly surprised to see works by world-class artists such as Henri Matisse, Pablo Picasso, and Pierre Bonnard in the collection.
I especially enjoyed the exhibitions featuring second- and third-generation Asian Australian female artists, as well as the collections by First Nations artists.
I think this museum is a great starting point for both art enthusiasts and beginners who want to learn more about Australian art and culture.
In addition to the main-floor restaurant, there is also a more casual café. We stopped there for a light snack. Both dining options offer lovely views overlooking the garden and the surrounding area. Outdoor seating is available as well, though I found the highway noise a bit distracting.Baguette D — Google reviewThe Art Gallery of New South Wales in Sydney is truly a world-class gallery, home to Renaissance paintings, contemporary works, powerful Aboriginal Australian art, and incredible local artists that will leave you inspired. Honestly, I couldn’t believe entry was free. If you love art and enjoy taking your time to wander through exhibitions, you could easily spend half a day here. Highly recommended.
What makes the experience even more special is the building itself — equally impressive, carrying the elegance and grandeur of a Victorian era that reflects the craftsmanship and architectural beauty of a time gone by.Authenticity S — Google reviewI recently visited the Art Gallery of New South Wales and had a fantastic experience. The gallery has an impressive collection of artworks ranging from classic Australian pieces to contemporary exhibitions, and the displays are beautifully presented. The building itself is stunning, with great views over the harbour and plenty of space to explore at your own pace. The staff were welcoming and knowledgeable, and best of all, general admission is free. It’s a wonderful place to spend a few hours whether you’re an art enthusiast or just looking for something interesting to do in Sydney.Ethan H — Google reviewAustralian art at its finest. From indigenous artwork to modern local artists through the renaissance period, this art museum had it all. Something for everyone truly. Enjoyed the outdoor sculpture garden portion as well.
Free entry! Lovely gift shop, elevator, escalator, and 3 floors of artwork! There is sometimes paid special exhibits which will require tickets and timed entry.S C — Google reviewThe Art Gallery of New South Wales is a premier cultural institution, beautifully situated on the edge of the Royal Botanic Garden. It brilliantly bridges the old and the new, with its grand, classical building seamlessly connected to the stunning contemporary architecture of the new Sydney Modern wing. Inside, the collection is vast and diverse, featuring everything from European old masters to an exceptional collection of Australian and Aboriginal art. It's a vibrant and inspiring space that invites you to wander and explore.Hang H — Google reviewI love this gallery! There’s so much art to see that you can easily lose track of time.
