SDR 3980 is a standalone, high-performance 8-channel Software Defined Radio (SDR) device equipped with 4× ADRV-9009 RF chips and an onboard Intel i9-9900K processor. Covering a frequency range of 75 MHz to 6 GHz, this platform delivers 450 MHz transmit and 200 MHz receive bandwidth, ideal for advanced wireless research and development.
Powered by a Xilinx Zynq UltraScale+ ZU11EG FPGA and running a built-in Ubuntu OS with GNU Radio support, it enables seamless prototyping and signal processing. The system also features:
8× TX and 8× RX channels
8× PCIe Gen3 interfaces, 1× SFP+ 10Gbps
64 GB DDR4 RAM and 500 GB SSD
Integrated APU, RPU, GPU for real-time control and processing
Multi-chip phase synchronization support
Size: 42 × 28 × 17.5 cm | Weight: 9.4 kg
This SDR is ideal for applications such as 5G NR simulation, 802.11 protocol emulation, beamforming, cognitive radio, RF sensing, and MIMO system development.
Host and data interfaces
The category spans USB-connected boards, Ethernet SDRs, and high-throughput PCIe/SFP systems. Existing Connector Type 95 is not equivalent because this feature represents supported data interfaces and may contain several values.
The sample clearly distinguishes 1, 2, 4, and 8 receive-channel platforms. Channel count is more informative than a redundant MIMO yes/no field.
RF transceiver chipset
RX maximum frequency
Maximum receive frequency varies substantially across the category and is a key shopper filter.
RX minimum frequency
All sampled products provide a lower receive-frequency boundary. The generic Frequency Range feature is not equivalent to an atomic numeric minimum.
Clocking and coherent synchronization are repeatedly specified and are important for MIMO and distributed-radio deployments. Multiple values can apply to one product.
Transmit-channel count is consistently stated and is important for MIMO, beamforming, and multi-signal applications.
TX maximum frequency
An atomic TX upper limit lets shoppers exclude receiver-only boards and compare transmitter coverage accurately.
TX minimum frequency
The transceiver products specify their operating ranges for TX/RX, while receiver-only products should leave this feature unset.
